nodecode-browserless is a rewrite of browserless's browserless/browserless-mcp:

  https://github.com/browserless/browserless-mcp
  commit 600a5d689fa3f5bdcb5676ffc063b56d18e9bd7c
  MIT License, Copyright (c) 2026 Browserless (LICENSE)

The plugin is a Hermes browser provider: for one browser task it launches a
Browserless cloud browser over CDP and starts a local multiplexer in front of
it, so Hermes' own browser tools drive that browser. This cell reaches the
same service a different way, because Nodecode's browser tools belong to
other cells: Browserless' REST endpoints, one call per page, registered as
three model tools. The launch options are the plugin's own and keep their
meaning — the stealth route (stealth on by default), the residential proxy
with its country and sticky IP, ad blocking, captcha solving, the session
timeout and the region endpoint — sent as the query string Browserless'
REST API reads them off, with the page's own waits in the JSON body. The
token is read where the plugin reads it (an environment variable, or a file
the operator owns), rides the query as the API takes it, and is taken back
out of anything the cell says. The plugin's HTTP status explanations, its
plan-timeout handling and its account page link are kept: a rejected token,
a concurrency limit and a refused render each say which of them it was.

Left out: the CDP multiplexer and the reconnect/session-API upstreams, which
exist so several CDP clients share one browser — a REST call is one page,
one connection, so there is nothing to multiplex, and Browserless closes the
browser itself; the `browserless-hermes-plugin/<version>` User-Agent tag,
because this cell is a different client of the same account; the "fall back
to local Chrome when a session cannot start" rule, which is Hermes' browser
selection and not a cell's, so a failed call answers why instead; the
`hermes plugins install` script and the plugin's manual smoke test; and the
click, type and navigate loop itself — the plugin supplies a browser to
Hermes' tools, while here each tool call is one whole page, so what the
model drives is the page's text, a screenshot or a PDF.
